method:  Method & Limitations How the visibility analysis actually works, where it can be wrong, and the published sources behind the guidance on this page. How the analysis works Everything runs client-side in JavaScript, entirely in your browser. The analyzer builds a luminance histogram from the image data, performs bright-spot and streak detection to locate sparkler trails, and assembles a 0–100 visibility score from three components: trail length, ember density, and a highlight-clipping penalty. EXIF metadata — shutter speed, ISO, aperture, and the flash-fired flag — is read locally from the file itself. Nothing is uploaded. There is no server, no accounts, and no AI or cloud vision is involved
footer:  Built as a static, client-side lab — no accounts, no uploads, no tracking. Imagery on this site is procedurally synthesized; see Method & Limitations. MIT licensed · source on GitHub 
hero:  A field guide + browser lab for night wedding exits Sparkler Exit Lab Why sparkler tunnels vanish in flash photos - and a browser lab to examine your shots and re-light the wands with customizable sparkler heads. Try the Lab See how it works 6 procedural heads 100% client-side 0 uploads 
